course: dictionary
lesson: 10433
title: The troposphere, the layer we live in
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The air around the Earth is called the atmosphere, and it is made of layers. The lowest layer is the troposphere, which reaches from the ground up to about 10 to 15 km. «Almost all weather happens in the lowest layer of the atmosphere, the troposphere.» The clues are lowest and weather: the troposphere is the bottom layer, where the weather happens.
explain: «In the troposphere the air usually gets colder the higher you go.» The clue is colder as you go higher, so the troposphere is the layer where the air cools as you climb. Check it by swapping in the bottom layer for troposphere. The sentence still makes sense, so your meaning fits.
explain: Science texts often name places in the same way. «Almost all the weather happens in the lowest layer, the troposphere, which is only about 12 kilometres thick.» The words only about 12 kilometres thick tell you it is a thin layer. The entry says 10 to 15 km, so 12 km fits.
fact: Troposphere means the lowest layer of the atmosphere, from the ground up to about 10 to 15 km.
fact: Almost all weather happens in the troposphere, where clouds, rain and wind form.
fact: In the troposphere the air usually gets colder the higher you go.
fact: Planes fly near the top of the troposphere.
fact: The layers of the atmosphere, from the ground up, are troposphere, stratosphere, mesosphere, thermosphere and exosphere.
exercise: «Almost all weather happens in the lowest layer of the atmosphere, the troposphere.» Where does almost all weather happen? | *In the troposphere | In the stratosphere | Under the sea | In space
exercise: «In the troposphere the air usually gets colder the higher you go.» What happens to the air as you climb in the troposphere? | It stays exactly the same | It turns into rain | It gets warmer | *It usually gets colder
exercise: Which layer of the atmosphere do we live in? | The mesosphere | *The troposphere | The stratosphere | The exosphere
test: «The troposphere is only about 12 kilometres thick.» How thick is the troposphere? | About 100 metres | About 500 km | About 1 metre | *About 10 to 15 km
test: «The air gets colder as you climb, which is why mountain tops are snowy.» According to this, why are mountain tops snowy? | Because snow only falls in summer | *Because the air gets colder as you climb | Because the sun is closer to them | Because mountains are made of ice
exam: Which list puts the layers of the atmosphere in the right order from the ground up? | Exosphere, mesosphere, troposphere | *Troposphere, stratosphere, mesosphere | Mesosphere, troposphere, thermosphere | Stratosphere, troposphere, exosphere
